taordatabaseobjects.ItusesanewdatabaseinitializercalledMigrateDatabaseToLatestVersion.TherearetwokindsofMigration:AutomatedDatabaseMigrationandCode-BasedDatabaseMigration¶¶Code-BasedMigrationinEntityFrameworkCodeFirstApproach -providesmorecontroloverthemigrationandallowsustoconfigureadditionalthingssuchassettingadefaultvalueofacolumn,configuringacomputedcolumn,etc.InordertouseCode-BasedMigrationinEntityFramework,weneedtoexecutethecommandsinthePackageManagerConsoleinVisualStudio.¶¶¶¶InheritanceandEntitytypehierarchymapping¶¶-Table-per-hierarchy (TPH)anddiscriminatorconfiguration —asingletableisusedtostoredataforalltypes,alongwithadiscriminatorcolumnthatdetermineswhichclasseachrowbelongsto.¶¶-Table-per-type (TPT)configuration —eachclasshasitsowntable.ThiscanbeimplementedusingattributesortheFluentAPI (`ToTable()`method),andjoinscanbeperformedusing `Include`.¶¶-Table-per-concrete-type (TPC)configuration —eachderivedclasshasitsowntable.Thesubclassesstoreboththeirownfieldsaswellasthoseinheritedfromthebaseclass.¶¶¶¶Defaulttransactionbehavior,Savepoints,externalDbTransactions?Каквыполнитьтранзакциюдлянесколькихконтекстов¶¶Bydefault,ifthedatabaseprovidersupportstransactions,allchangesinasinglecalltoSaveChangesareappliedinatransaction.Ifanyofthechangesfail,thenthetransactionisrolledbackandnoneofthechangesareappliedtothedatabase.¶Youcanalsowrapablockofcodeinatransactionexplicitly,andtocommitityoumustcall:¶usingvartransaction =context.Database.BeginTransaction();¶transaction.Commit();¶¶Savepoints -whenSaveChangesisinvoked¹andatransactionisalreadyinprogress²onthecontext,EFautomaticallycreatesasavepointbeforesavinganydata.Savepointsarepointswithin³adatabasetransactionwhichmaylaterberolledbackto,ifanerroroccursorforanyotherreason.IfSaveChangesencounters⁴anyerror,itautomaticallyrollsthetransactionbacktothesavepoint,leavingthetransactioninthesamestateasifithadneverstarted. (вызывается,выполняется,в,встречает)¶try =>transaction.CreateSavepoint(\"BeforeMoreBlogs\");¶catch (Exception) =>transaction.RollbackToSavepoint(\"BeforeMoreBlogs\");¶¶ExternalDbTransactions —allowusingmultipledataaccesstechnologieswithinthesamerelationaldatabasetransaction.¶Toshareasingle `DbConnection`,youneedtopasstheconnectiontothecontextwhenitiscreated.Insteadofusingthe `OnConfiguring`method,usetheconstructorwith `DbContextOptions<T>`,oraccepta `DbConnection`intheconstructor,storeitinaparameter,andthenuseitin `DbContext.OnConfiguring`.¶¶¶¶Messaging.ЧтотакоеAMQP?QueuevsTopic,Exchangesиеготипы.¶¶AMQP (AdvancedMessageQueuingProtocol) —aprotocolfortransferringmessagesbetweenindependentcomponentsindistributedsystems.¶¶ThedifferencebetweenaQueueandaTopicisthatmessagessenttoaqueuearedeliveredtoonlyoneconsumer (Point-to-Point),whilemessagespublishedtoatopicaredeliveredtomultipleconsumerssimultaneously (Publish-and-Subscribe)¶¶ExchangesinAMQP:¶Messagesarenotpublisheddirectlytoqueues;instead,theyarefirstsenttoanexchange,whichthenusesbindingstoroutethemtoqueues.¶¶TypesofExchanges (Direct,Fanout,Topic,Headers,System):¶-Direct —deliversmessagestoqueuesbasedontheroutingkeyattribute.Usedforpoint-to-pointdelivery.¶-Fanout —broadcastsmessagedeliveringtoallboundqueues,ignoringtheroutingkey.¶-Topic —deliversmessagestoqueuesbasedontheroutingkeyandapattern (regularexpression).Suitablefordeliveringmessagesdependingonthedatacategory.¶-Headers —similartoTopic,butroutingisbasedonkey-valuepairsratherthantheroutingkey.Usedwhenroutinglogicismorecomplexandbasedonmultipleattributes.¶-Systemexchanges — ????¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеAzureServiceBus,вчемразницамеждуServiceBusиобычнойQueue?ReceiveAndDeleteиPeekLock¶¶4¶¶¶¶ВчемотличиеKafkaотServiceBusилидругогоброкерасообщений?безответа¶¶1¶¶¶¶Способыкоммуникациимеждумикросервисами.​Saga,eventualconsistency.Two-PhaseCommit.¶¶Communicationmethods:RESTAPI,gRPC,MessageBroker (AMQP),SignalR.Therearealso:MQTT,STOMP,andServiceMesh.¶¶Sagaisapatternformanagingtransactionsandensuringdataconsistencyacrossmicroservices.Itisusedwheneachservicehasitsowndatabaseandatransactionspansmultipleservices.Toensuredataconsistencybetweenservices,eachrequesthasacompensatingrequestthatisexecutediftheoriginalrequestfails.Thiscanbeimplementedintwoways:¶¶-Choreography —thereisnotransactioncoordinationservice;eachservicesubscribestoeventsfromotherservicesanddecidesforitselfwhattodonext.¶¶-Orchestration —thereisatransactioncoordinationservicethattakesresponsibilityfordecision-makingandthesequenceofbusinesslogicexecution.¶¶UnlikeTwo-PhaseCommit,Sagafirstperformsthecommit,andthen,incaseoffailure,startsthecompensationprocess.Two-PhaseCommitsplitstheprocessintotwophases:thepreparephaseandthecommitphase.First,itasksifthenodesarereadytoperformthetransaction,andthenasksthemtocommit;ifatleastonenoderespondsnegatively,itasksalltorollback.Instead,Sagabreaksthetransactionintostepsthatcanberolledbackindependently.¶Sagaprovideseventualconsistencyratherthanstrictatomicity,anditscalesbetterinmicroservicesenvironmentssinceservicesremaindecoupled.¶¶Eventualconsistencyisapatternfordataconsistencywheredataisupdatedwithsomedelay,buteventuallyallnodeswillbeconsistent,althoughwithsomelag (forexample,aDNSserver).¶¶¶¶Какиепаттерныдлямикросервисоввызнаете?Какобеспечитьsecurityмикросервисов?Какиеспособыхостинга -нетответа¶¶1¶¶¶¶КакиеTestFrameworksвызнаете?Какиевспомогательныебиблиотекивызнаетеииспользовалидлянаписаниятестов?¶¶NUnit —supportsparameterizedtestsandiseasytouse.Attributesformarkingtestsinclude `[Test]`, `[SetUp]`,and `[TearDown]`.¶¶xUnit —workswellwithasynchronousoperationsandmultithreading.ConsideredmoremodernandflexiblecomparedtoNUnit.Doesnotrequire `[SetUp]`and `[TearDown]`attributes —instead,constructorsanddestructorsareused.¶¶MSTest —thebuilt-inframeworkfromMicrosoft,tightlyintegratedwithVisualStudioandusedwithintheMicrosoftecosystem.¶¶Moq —alibraryforcreatingmocksandtestinginteractionsbetweenobjects.¶¶FluentAssertions —improvesthereadabilityofassertionsbyprovidingexpressive,human-readablesyntaxforconditionchecks.¶¶AutoFixture —automatesdatagenerationfortests,makingobjectinitializationeasier.¶¶FakeItEasyandNSubstitute —alternativestoMoq.Botharelibrariesforcreatingmocks,offeringasimpleinterfaceforquicklywritingtests.¶¶¶¶ВчемразницамеждуIntegrationиUnitтестами?¶¶Integrationtestsareusedtotestrealsystemmodules (database,service,etc.) —notmocks—andtochecktheirinteractionwitheachother (registration,purchasingaproduct,etc.).However,theywillnotshowtheexactlocationofafailure.Unittestshelpwiththis,soitisimportanttohavegoodunittestcoverage.Whenthesystemislargeandthereisnotenoughtimetocovereverythingwithunittests,integrationtestscanbeatemporarysolutionuntilthesystemisfullycovered.¶¶¶¶ВчемразницамеждуDummy,Fake,Stubs,Spies,Mocksобъектами?¶¶3¶¶¶¶Чтопроисходит,когдапользовательвбиваетвбраузерадресgoogle.com¶¶1¶¶¶¶Протоколы:HTTP,HTTP/2,HTTP/3,HTTPS.СтруктураHTTP-сообщения¶¶HTTP (HyperTextTransferProtocol)isaprotocolforexchangingdatabetweenaclientandaserver.ItwasoriginallydesignedforretrievinghypertextdocumentsinHTMLformat,butnowisusedforanyresources.¶HTTP/2 —animprovedversionofHTTP/1.1withthefollowingmainchanges:¶-Multiplexing —allowssendingmultiplerequestsandresponsessimultaneouslyoverasingleTCPconnection.¶-Headercompressionandbinaryformat —reducesdatasizeandspeedsuptransmission.¶-Requestprioritization —optimizestheloadingofimportantresources.¶-ServerPush —theservercansendadditionalresourcestotheclientbeforetheyarerequested,improvingperformance.¶-HTTPS (HTTPwithencryption)isusedwithHTTP/2forsecuredatatransmission.¶¶HTTP/3 (basedonQUIC —QuickUDPInternetConnections) —unlikethefirstandsecondversions,itworksoverUDP/IPinsteadofTCP/IP.¶-Improvedmultiplexing —ifonerequestfails,onlythatspecificrequestneedstoberetransmitted (insteadofblockingtheentireconnectionasinTCP).¶-Fasterencryption —becauseQUICcombinesthehandshakeandconnectionsetupintoonestep.¶TherearetwotypesofHTTPmessages:requestsandresponses.¶Request:¶-HTTPmethod,URI —thepathtotheresource,andtheprotocolversion:¶GET /index.htmlHTTP/1.1¶-Headers:provideadditionalinformationtotheserver.Examples: `Host` (domain,tospecifywhichsitetheclientistryingtoaccess), `Cookie`, `Authorization` (forauthentication).¶-Body:formethodslikePOST,containsdatasenttotheserver (separatedbyablankline).¶Response:¶-HTTPprotocolversion,andstatuscode (e.g., `200OK` —successfulrequest)¶HTTP/1.1 200OK¶-Headers:similartorequestheaders.¶-Body:optional,containsthereturnedresource (separatedbyablankline).¶¶¶¶СтруктураURL-адреса,URI,АбсолютныеиотносительныеURL¶¶URLstructure:http://blog.example.com:80/catalog/category/knifes?key1=value1&amp;amp;key2=value2#anchor¶¶ -http:// —protocol;¶ -blog —subdomain (third-leveldomain);¶ -example —second-leveldomain;¶ -com —top-leveldomain (TLD);¶ - 80 —port;¶ - /catalog/category/knifes —pathtotheresource;¶ -key1=value1&key2=value2 —GETrequestparameters (querystring);¶ - #anchor —anchor,alinktoaspecificplaceonthepage.¶¶URI (UniformResourceIdentifier) —ageneraltermthatincludesbothURL (UniformResourceLocator)andURN (UniformResourceName).¶¶AbsoluteURL:specifiesthefullpathtoaresource,includingprotocolanddomain.Example: `http://example.com/page`.¶¶RelativeURL:specifiesthepathrelativetothecurrentresourceorbaseURL.Example: /ru/docs/Learn.¶¶Anchors:allowyoutolinknotjusttoanHTMLpage,buttoaspecificplaceonthepage.¶¶¶¶КакойHTTPметодявляетсяидемпотентным.МетодыHTTP-запросовистатускоды.¶¶AnHTTPmethodisidempotentifrepeatingthesamerequestoneormoretimesinarowhasthesameeffectanddoesnotchangetheserverstate.Forexample,POSTaddsanewobjecteachtimeandisnotidempotent,whileGET,PUT,DELETE,PATCH,andHEADareidempotent.¶¶-GET —retrievesinformationaboutaresource.Canbecached: 200, 400, 404¶-POST —createsaresource: 200, 201, 204, 304¶-PUT —completelyreplacesaresource: 200, 204¶-DELETE —deletesaresource: 200, 204¶-PATCH —editsaresource,usedtoupdateonlyonefield: 200¶-HEAD —doesnotretrievethebody,butsendsandreceivesHTTPheaders: 200¶-OPTIONS —usedtodeterminewhichrequestHTTPmethodsaresupportedbyaserver.¶-CONNECT —establishesatwo-wayconnectiontotherequestedresource.CanbeusedtoaccessasitethatusesHTTPS.¶-TRACE —usedtoperformaloopback \"echo\"test.Itallowstheclienttosendarequesttotheserver,whichreturnsthesamerequestbacktotheclient.Usefulfordebuggingandtesting.¶¶CONNECTandTRACEmethodscancausesecurityissues,andmanyserversandproxyserversrestrictordisabletheirusebydefault.¶¶Statuscodes:¶ 1XX -Informational; `100Continue`, `101SwitchingProtocols`¶ 2XX -Success; 200 -Requestsuccessful; 201Created; 204NoContent;¶ 3XX -Redirection; 301 -MovedPermanently; 302 -MovedTemporarily; 304NotModified;¶ 4XX -ClientError; 400Badrequest ; 401Unauthorized; 403Forbidden; 404Notfound; 408 -Requesttimeout; 418I\'mateapot; 498InvalidToken;¶ 5XX -ServerError; 500 -Internalservererror; 502BadGateway; 503ServiceUnavailable; 504 -Gatewaytimeout.¶¶¶¶WebSockets,особенностииосновныеWebSocketAPIevents?SignalR¶¶WebSocketsisanetworkprotocolthatprovidesareal-time,bidirectionalconnectionbetweenclientandserveroverasingleTCPconnection,untilitisclosedbyeitherside.Itisidealforchats,onlinegames,tradingplatforms,notifications,etc.¶¶Advantages:¶-Fullduplex:Bothsidescansendmessagesindependentlyofeachother,unlikeHTTP,wheretheclientisalwaystheinitiator.¶-Provideslowlatencycommunication,asitmaintainsapersistentopenconnection.¶-Noadditionalhandshakesarerequiredforeachmessage.¶¶Howdoesitwork?¶Initially,ahandshakeisperformedviaaregularHTTP(S)requestwiththe `Upgrade:websocket`header.TheserverconfirmstheswitchtotheWebSocketprotocol.Afterthat,bothsidescannowexchangemessagesinrealtime.¶¶MainWebSocketAPIeventsinclude:¶-open:theconnectionisestablished;¶-message:amessageisreceivedfromtheotherside;¶-error:aconnectionerroroccurs;¶-close:theconnectionisclosed.¶¶SignalRisalibraryforASP.NETCorethatprovidesreal-timecommunicationandbaseonWebSocket,soithasallthesameadvantages.However,italsosupportsprotocolslikeServer-SentEvents (SSE)orLongPollingifWebSocketisnotavailable.SignalRautomaticallyselectsthebestpossibletransportprotocolforcommunication.¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеSSE,ShortpollingиLongpolling?¶¶WebSocket —alreadydiscussedearlier,aswellasSignalR.¶¶Server-SentEvents (SSE) —atechnologyforone-waydatatransmissionfromservertoclientinrealtimeoverastandardHTTPconnection.Itworksonlyinonedirection:server →client.¶¶Longpolling —theclientsendsarequesttotheservertoopenaconnection.Theserverkeepstheconnectionopenuntilithasdatatosendinresponse.Aftertheserversendsaresponse,theclientimmediatelysendsanewrequesttokeeptheconnectionalive.¶¶Shortpolling —theclientitselfsendsGETrequeststotheserveratregularintervalstoreceiveupdates.¶¶LongpollingandShortpollingareoutdatedtechnologiesusedtosimulatereal-timecommunicationwhenServer-SentEventsorWebSocketarenotavailable.¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеHATEOAS,вчемегоосновнаяидеяипреимущества?¶¶HATEOAS (HypermediaastheEngineofApplicationState)isanarchitecturalprincipleofRESTAPIs,wheretheclientreceivesnotonlydatabutalsohypermedia—i.e.,linkstorelatedresourcesandavailableactions —allowingnavigationthroughtheapplicationjustlikefollowinglinksontheweb.Thisway,theclientonlyneedstoknowthebaseURLofthewebservice,andallnecessaryinformationisprovideddynamicallybytheserver.¶¶AdvantagesofHATEOAS:¶-Increasestheflexibilityandresilience —changingtheURLstructuredoesnotbreakclientcode.¶-Reducescouplingandpromotesdecompositionoftheapplicationintosmallerservices.¶-Self-describingAPI —theclientcanunderstandwhatcanbedonewitharesourcewithoutdocumentation.¶¶Example:¶[¶ \"id\": 123,¶ \"name\": \"JohnDoe\",¶ \"status\": \"Active\",¶ \"links\": [¶ [ \"rel\": \"self\", \"href\": \"/users/123\" ],¶ [ \"rel\": \"update\", \"href\": \"/users/123\", \"method\": \"PUT\" ],¶ [ \"rel\": \"delete\", \"href\": \"/users/123\", \"method\": \"DELETE\" ],¶ [ \"rel\": \"orders\", \"href\": \"/users/123/orders\" ]¶ ]¶]¶¶¶¶CORS¶¶CORS (Cross-OriginResourceSharing)isabrowsersecuritymechanismthatallowswebpagestorequestresourcesfromadifferentdomainthantheonefromwhichthesiteitselfwasloaded.ThepurposeofCORSistopreventawebsitefrommakingunauthorizedrequeststodomainsitdoesn\'tcontrol.WithoutCORS,suchattemptsareblockedbytheSame-OriginPolicy (SOP),whichrestrictsaccesstoresponsesfromdifferentorigins.Importantly,the **requestisstillsent**,buttheresponsebecomesinaccessibletotheclientduetoSOPrestrictions.Theoriginisdefinedasacombinationofprotocol (https),host (example.com),port (443),etc.¶¶Toallowauseragent (browser)toaccessaresourcefromanotherdomain,CORSmustbeenabledintheresponseheadersontheserverside `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.¶CORSdoes **not**protectagainst **CSRF (Cross-SiteRequestForgery)**,whereanattackerforgesrequestsusingthecredentialsofanauthenticateduser.ToprotectagainstCSRF,othermechanismsareused,suchasCSRFtokensandCustomauthenticationheaders.¶¶¶¶HTTPcache.Privatecache.Publiccache/Sharedcache.Cache-Control,ETag.СпособыкэшированиявASP.NETCore?¶¶Cachingisamechanismthatreducesthenumberofidenticalrequeststotheserverandincreasesthespeedofcontentdeliverytoclients.¶¶-PublicCache (sharedcache) —acachethatcanbeusedbymultipleclients.Forexample,aproxyserveroraContentDeliveryNetwork (CDN),whichisadistributednetworkofserverslocatedclosertousers.¶¶-Privatecache —acacheintendedforasingleuser.¶¶HTTPheadersforcaching:¶-Cache-Control —aheaderthattellsthebrowserhowandforhowlongtocachearesource.Attributes: `public`, `private`, `no-cache`, `no-store`, `max-age`,etc.¶¶-ETag (EntityTag) —auniqueidentifierfortheversionofaresource (string,hash)generatedbytheserver.Theclientsavesthetagandsendsitwiththenextrequest;iftheresourcehasn\'tchanged,theserverreturns `304NotModified`,andtheclientusesitscachedcopy.¶¶CachingmethodsinASP.NETCore:¶-ResponseCacheAttribute —specifiescachingparametersfor `Cache-Control`andotherheaders,butdoesnotitselfcacheresponses.¶¶-ResponseCachingMiddleware —unlikethepreviousmethod,itcachesresponseswithintheASP.NETCoreapplication.¶¶-In-memorycache (IMemoryCache) —representsacachestoredinthememoryofthewebserver.Youneedtoensure \"stickysessions\"sothatrequestsfromaclientalwaysgotothesameserver.¶¶ -Distributedcaching -isacachesharedbymultipleappserverssuchasRedis,NCache;¶-Hybridcache —combinesbothapproaches:dataisfirstretrievedfromthein-memorycache,andifnotfound,fromthedistributedcache.¶¶-Outputcaching —anewstandardfor .NET 7+versions,whichisbetterthantheResponseCacheAttribute/Middleware.¶¶¶¶REST.КакиетребованиекRESTfulсервису?RichardsonMaturityModel (RMM)¶¶REST (RepresentationalStateTransfer)isanarchitecturalstyleforinteractionbetweensystemcomponentsoveranetwork.Itisasetofrulesforwritingserver-sideapplicationcodethatsimplifiesscalinganddataexchangebetweensystems.RESTwasproposedbyRoyFieldingin 2000,whoisalsoaco-authoroftheHTTPandURIstandards.¶¶ARESTfulserviceisaservicethatfullycomplieswithRESTprinciples.ThemainprinciplesofRESTare:¶ -Client-serverarchitecture -thesystemmustbeseparatedintoclientsandservers;¶¶ -Statelessness -theserverdoesnotstoreclientstate;allnecessaryinformationissentineachrequest;¶¶ -Caching -responsesmustexplicitlyindicatewhethertheyarecacheable (`Cache-Control`, `ETag`);¶¶ -Uniforminterface:¶ -EachresourceisaccessibleviaauniqueURI.¶ -Dataexchangedincommonformats (typicallyJSONorXML).¶ -CommunicationthroughstandardHTTPmethods;¶¶ -HATEOASsupport —clientsdiscoveravailableactionsthroughhypermedialinks.¶¶-Layeredsystem —thesystemcanbedividedintolayers,whereeachlayerinteractsonlywithitsimmediateneighbors.¶¶-Code-On-Demand —theservercansendexecutablecodefortheclienttorun¶¶AdvantagesofRESTfulservices:improvescalability,security,andisolationofcomponents,andalsomakedeploymenteasier.¶¶RichardsonMaturityModel (RMM):showshowcloselyanAPIfollowsREST:¶-Level 0:OneURIandoneHTTPmethod (e.g.,POST).¶-Level 1:MultipleURIs,butstillonlyoneHTTPmethod.¶-Level 2:MultipleURIsandfulluseofHTTPmethods.¶-Level 3:HATEOAS.APIsdescribetheircapabilitiesandrelationshipsthroughhyperlinks,makingiteasiertochangeURIswithoutchangingclients.¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеRPCAPI,RESTAPIикакаяразницамеждуними?КакиереализацииRPCпротоколавызнаете?GraphQL¶¶RESTAPIandRPCAPIaretwopopularAPIarchitecturesthatallowclient-serverinteraction.BothuseHTTPastheunderlyingprotocol,andthemessageformatsareusuallyJSONandXML.¶¶RESTAPIallowsyoutoperformspecificoperationswithdataonaremoteserverusingHTTPmethods.TheclientsendsarequesttotheserverataspecificURIwithanHTTPmethod,andtheserverreturnstheresultoftheoperation.¶¶-RPCAPI —allowscallingremotefunctionsonexternalserversasiftheywerelocaltoyourapplication.¶¶ThereareseveraldifferentimplementationsoftheRPC (RemoteProcedureCall)protocol:¶-XML-RPC —usesXMLintheHTTPrequestbody.¶-JSON-RPC —usesJSONintheHTTPrequestbody.¶-SOAP —anextensionofXML-RPC,supportingmorecomplexmessagingintheSOAP-XMLformat.¶-gRPC:developedbyGoogle.ItusesbinaryProtobufserializationfordatatransfer.Requires `.proto`contractstodefineAPImethods.WorksoverHTTP/2.¶¶GraphQL —developedbyFacebook (2015).Allowstheclienttorequestonlythedataitneeds,ratherthantheentireobject.Thereisonlyoneendpointforallrequests,andtheschemastructureisdefinedontheserverside.RequestsaredividedintoQuery (GET),Mutation (POST,PUT),andSubscription (real-timeupdates).Drawbacks:nosupportforstandardHTTPerrorcodes,caching,N+1problem,securityrisks (fieldsnotintendedforpublicaccesscanbeexposed),andtheschemacanbecomeverylarge.¶-RESTisbettersuitedforweb/publicAPIs.¶-gRPC/RPCisbettersuitedforservice-to-servicecommunicationwithinmicroservicearchitectures.¶¶¶¶АутентификациииАвторизации,видытокенов.¶¶Authenticationistheprocessofverifyingtherighttoaccessbyenteringausernameandpassword.¶¶Authorizationistheprocessofcheckingwhatrightsandprivilegesareavailabletotheuser,suchasrolesandpermissionsincontrollers.¶¶AuthN =Name,AuthZ =Zone.¶Authentication = *Whoareyou?*andAuthorization = *Whatareyouallowedtodo?*¶¶-IDtoken —theresultofauthentication,containingtheuser\'sidentifierandinformationabouttheauthenticationtime.Itcanbeextendedwithcustomuserdata.¶¶-Accesstoken —allowsaccesstoAPIresources.ClientsrequestaccesstokensandsendthemtotheAPI.Itcontainsinformationabouttheclientanduser,andtheAPIusesthisinformationtoauthorizeaccesstodata.¶¶-Refreshtoken —anoptionaltokenthatcanbereturnedtorefreshtheaccesstokenwithoutre-authenticating.¶¶¶¶OAuth2иRoles,OpenIDConnect,IdentityServer.¶¶OAuth2isanauthorizationprotocolthatallowsthird-partyapplicationstoaccessprotectedresourcesandperformactionsforauserwithoutpassingtheirlogin/password.Roles:¶-Client —thethird-partyapplication (Reactapp).¶-ResourceOwner -theuser;¶-ResourceServer —theserverthatcontainstheowner\'sresources (WebAPI);¶-AuthorizationServer —theserverthatissuesaccesstokenstoclientsforaccessingtheResourceServer.¶¶TheclientrequestsaccesstoresourcesownedbytheResourceOwnerandlocatedontheResourceServer.Accessisgrantednotbyloginandpassword,butbyatokenissuedbytheAuthorizationServer.¶¶OpenIDConnect (OIDC)isanextensionoftheOAuth2protocolthataddsanauthenticationmechanism,allowingclientstoidentifytheuserandobtainbasicprofileinformationviaanIDtoken.OIDCisalsousedtoimplementSingleSign-On (SSO),allowinguserstosignintodifferentsitesandapplicationsusingasingleaccount (viaGoogle).¶¶OAuth2vsOIDC:¶-OAuth2providesonlyauthorization (whatyouareallowedtodo).¶-OIDCextendsOAuth2andaddsauthentication (whoareyou?)andbasicprofiledata.¶ -Therequestincludestheattribute `scope=openid`;¶ -TheresponseincludesID,access,andrefreshtokens.¶¶IdentityServerisanauthorizationserverthatimplementstheOIDCandOAuth2standards.Theuser (ResourceOwner)allowstheClienttoauthenticatebyenteringtheirloginandpassword.IdentitySerververifiesthepassword,theauthenticityoftheclientanduser,andissuestheclientID,access,andrefreshtokens.Theauthorizationprocessiscomplete.TheclientthenrequestsdatafromtheResourceServer (WebAPI),providingtheaccesstokenforauthorization.TheWebAPIchecksthetokenanddecideswhethertograntaccess.¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеJWTtoken?Изкакихчастейсостоит?ЗачемнуженРефрештокен?КакинвалидироватьJWTтокенвASP.NET?¶¶JSONWebToken (JWT)isasecurewaytotransmitinformationbetweenaclientandaserverusingencodingandhashing.AJWTconsistsofthreeparts: `header.payload.signature`:¶-Header —containsthetokentype (`JWT`)andthehashingalgorithmusedforthesignature (e.g., `SHA256`, `RS256`).¶-Payload (Claims) —containsthedata (claims)necessaryforuserauthorization.¶-Signature —asignaturecreatedbyencodingtheHeaderandPayloadusingbase64url,thenhashingthemwiththealgorithmspecifiedintheHeaderandasecretkeytoguaranteedataintegrity.¶¶JWTverification —whentheserverreceivesatoken,itperformsthesamesignaturecalculationandcomparesittothereceivedsignature.¶¶Refreshtoken (RT) —isusedtoobtainanewaccesstoken.Refreshtokensarelong-livedbutsingle-use.WhentheoldJWTexpires,yousendtheRTandreceiveanewpairofJWT+RT.WiththenewJWT,youcanagainaccessprivateresources.¶Refreshtokensallowclientstorefreshaccesstokenswithoutrequiringtheusertologinagain,althoughtheymuststillcontacttheauthorizationserver.¶¶HowtoinvalidateaJWTtoken:Itisimpossibletoknowifithasbeencompromised,butyoucanchangethesecretkey,reduceitslifetime,oraddittoablacklist.¶-IfsomeonestealsonlytheAccessTokenfromtheclientapplication,itwillexpirewithinitsshortlifespan.¶-Ifsomeonestealsbothtokens,afteranhouronecopyoftheapplicationwillsuccessfullygetanewpairoftokens,andtheotherwillberejected,sincethatRefreshTokennolongerexistsinthedatabase.Theuserwillthennoticetheyhavebeenloggedoutandmaysuspectsomethingiswrong.¶¶¶¶Расскажитепровеб-серверыASP.NETCore?¶¶Inthepast, .NETapplicationswerehosteddirectlyontheIISwebserver.Butsince .NETCoresupportscross-platformdevelopment,directhostingonIISwasabandoned.NowIISisprimarilyusedasareverseproxy.InASP.NETCore,HTTPserverslikeHTTP.sysandKestrelhandleincomingHTTPrequests,passingthemintothe `HttpContext`andthenfurtherdowntheapplication\'smiddlewarepipeline.¶¶-HTTP.sys —awebserverthatworksonlyonWindows.ItissuitablewhenyouneedWindowsAuthenticationsupportorotherWindows-specificfeatures.¶¶-Kestrel —across-platformwebserverusedbydefaultinASP.NETCore.Itcanrunstandaloneorincombinationwithreverseproxies (IIS,Nginx,Apache).ReverseproxiesreceiverequestsandforwardthemtoKestrel,allowingyoutousetheiradvancedfeatures,suchashidingapplicationportsandloadbalancing,SSLterminationandflexibletrafficroutingtodifferentIPsandports.Withoutaproxy,Kestrelhandlesalltrafficdirectlyontheport,ignoringtheHostheader.¶¶-IIS —isusedasaproxyserverforASP.NETCoreapplicationsrunningonKestrel.IthandlesincomingtrafficandforwardsittoKestrel,providingadditionalmanagementfeaturessuchasloadbalancing,SSL,andtrafficrouting.¶¶¶¶ASP.NETCore.ЧтотакоеmiddlewareвASP.NETCore?Созданиеидобавлениевpipeline.¶¶ASP.NETCoreisacross-platform,open-sourcedevelopmentframeworkforbuildingcloudapplications.¶¶InASP.NETCore,middlewareisclassthatrepresentstheHTTPrequestandresponseprocessingpipeline.Eachmiddlewareprocessestheincomingrequestandthenpassesittothenextoneforfurtherprocessing,whiletheresponseisprocessedinthereverseorder.Theorderofmiddlewarecomponentsaffectsthesecurityandperformanceoftheapplication.Bydefault,ASP.NETCoreprovidesanumberofbuilt-inmiddlewaresuchas:ExceptionHandler,Hsts,HttpsRedirection,StaticFiles,Routing,Cors,Authentication,Authorization,etc.¶¶Waystoaddmiddleware:¶-Creatingcustommiddleware (class +RequestDelegatenextfield +InvokeAsync(HttpContext).Toaddthecustommiddlewaretothepipeline,youcanuse `UseMiddleware<T>`withthetypeofthecreatedclass,orcreateanextensionmethodfor `IApplicationBuilder`thatreturns `builder.UseMiddleware<T>`,andthenusethisextensionmethodinthepipeline.¶-IMiddlewareisaninterfaceforcreatingmiddleware,allowsyoutoinjectdependenciesfromDI,anditisalsoregisteredthroughDI,soyoucansetthelifetimeasscoped/transient.¶-Inlinemiddleware (delegates)isthesimplestandfastestway:usedelegatesdirectlyinthepipeline. `app.Use(delegate)`.¶¶¶¶Run,Use,Next,Map.Вчемразницамеждуapp.Useиapp.Runпридобавленииmiddleware?¶¶Run() —isaterminalmiddleware,usedattheendofthepipelinebecauseitdoesnotcall `next` (thenextmiddlewareinthepipeline).Evenifyouaddasecond `Run`,onlythefirstonewillbeexecuted.¶¶Use() —addsmiddlewaretothepipeline.Ittakes `HttpContext`and `next`,andcanexecutelogicbothbeforeandaftercalling `next()`.¶¶Next() —isa `RequestDelegate`delegatethatcallsthenextmiddlewareinthepipeline.Ifyoudon\'tcall `next()`,thepipelinewillendatthispoint.¶¶Map(path, ...) —usedtobranchthemiddlewarepipelinebasedonaspecificURLpath: `app.Map(\"/UrlPath\",MapCustomMiddleware);`¶¶MapWhen(predicate, ...) —usedtobranchbasedonaspecificpredicatecondition (`Func<HttpContext,bool>`).Forexample: `context =>context.Request.Query.ContainsKey(\"id\");`¶¶UseWhen(predicate, ...) —alsotakesapredicate,butafterexecutingthespecifiedmiddleware,itreturnsbacktothemainpipeline.¶¶UsevsRun — `Use`allowsyoutocallthenextmiddlewareinthepipeline,while `Run`doesnot,asitdoesnottakea `next`parameter.Afterreaching `Run`,controlreturnstothepreviousmiddleware,whichchecksifthereisanycodeafter `next()`;ifso,itexecutesitandthenpassescontroltothenextmiddleware.¶¶Whathappensifyoudon\'tcall `next()`inside `Use`? -Thechainwillbreakatthispoint,andnofurthermiddleware/endpointswillbeexecuted.¶¶¶¶Типыmiddleware.¶¶Middlewarecanbedividedintothefollowingtypes:¶-Request-EditingMiddleware —editstherequest.¶¶-Short-CircuitingMiddleware —filtersrequestsbasedonacertaincondition;eitherreturnsaresponseimmediatelyorforwardstherequestfurtherdowninthepipeline.¶¶-Content-GeneratingMiddleware —generatestheresponseitselfwithinthecurrentmiddlewareandsendsitbacktotheclientwithoutcallingthenextmiddleware.¶¶-Response-EditingMiddleware —editstheresponse.¶¶¶¶ЧтотакоеAttributeroutingвASP.NETCore?¶¶Attributeroutingisamechanismthatallowsyoutodefineroutesdirectlyincontrollersandactionmethodsusingattributes,insteadofaglobalrouteconfiguration.¶¶ApiController —anattributeappliedonlytocontrollers.Itenablesautomaticmodelvalidation,requiredrouteparameters,andautomatic `[FromBody]`binding.¶¶Attributesappliedonlytoactionmethods: `[HttpGet]`, `[HttpPost]`, `[HttpPut]`, `[HttpPatch]`, `[HttpDelete]`, `[HttpHead]`, `[HttpOptions]`¶¶Attributesappliedonlytoactionmethodparameters:¶¶-FromQuery —readsquerystringparameters (`?key=value`).CommonlyusedinGETrequests.Ifthekeymatchestheparametername, `[FromQuery]`canbeomitted.¶ [HttpGet]¶publicActionResultGetAccount([FromQuery]intid);¶publicActionResultGetAccount([FromQuery(Name = \"id\")]intkey);¶¶-FromRoute —readsthevaluefromthelastroutesegment\'/\'ofthepath (`/api/accounts/5`).CommonlyusedinGETrequests.Canbeomittediftheparameternamematchesthetemplatein `[placeholder]`.¶ [HttpGet(\"[id]\")]¶publicActionResultGetAccount(intid)¶¶-FromBody —readsdatafromtheHTTPrequestbodyinJSONformat.CommonlyusedinPOST/PUTrequests.¶ [HttpPost]/[HttpPut]¶publicActionResultCreateAccount([FromBody]AccountModelaccount)¶¶-FromHeader —readsdatafromtheHTTPheader.CommonlyusedinGETrequests.¶ [HttpGet]¶publicActionResultGetAccount([FromHeader]stringauthorization)¶¶-FromForm —readsformdata (`x-www-form-urlencoded`or `multipart/form-data`).CommonlyusedinPOSTrequests.¶ [HttpPost]¶publicActionResultUploadFile([FromForm]IFormFilefile)¶¶-FromServices —injectsadependencydirectlyintotheactionmethodwithoutconstructorinjection.¶publicIActionResultDoWork([FromServices]IMyServicesvc) =>Ok(svc.Do());¶¶¶¶ВчемразницамеждуConfigureServicesиConfigureвASP.NETCore?Чтотакоеwwwroot?¶¶ConfigureServicesisusedtoregisterservicesintheintegratedDIcontainer.HereyoucanaddEntityFrameworkin `ConfigureServices`anduseitbyinjectingitintoconstructorsorotherrequiredservices,suchasdatabasesorauthentication.Whenregisteringservices,youcandefinetheirlifetime (Singleton,Scoped,Transient),whichaffectshowinstancesarecreatedandusedintheapplication.¶¶publicvoidConfigureServices(IServiceCollectionservices)[services.Add ]¶¶TheConfiguremethodisusedtodefinethemiddlewareHTTPrequestandresponseprocessingpipeline.¶¶publicvoidConfigure(IApplicationBuilderapp,IWebHostEnvironmentenv)[app.UseRouting();]¶¶wwwrootisthedefaultfolderinASP.NETCoreforstoringstaticfilessuchasCSS,JavaScript,images,fonts,andotherresourcesthatshouldbedirectlyaccessibletoclientsviathewebserver.Thenameofthisfoldercanbechangedusing `UseWebRoot`.¶¶Inthelatestversionof .NET, `ConfigureServices`and `Configure`havebeenreplacedby `builder.Services.Add...`and `app.Use...`directlyin `Program.cs`insteadof `Startup.cs`.¶¶¶¶КакнаписатьинтеграционныйтествASP.NETCore?¶¶Towriteanintegrationtest,youcanusethe `TestServer`classfromthe `Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost`library,whichallowsyoutorunalightweightin-memoryserverwithminimalconfiguration,andwithoutdeployingthefullapplication.Thisway,youcansendHTTPrequestsandreceiveresponsesinordertotestvariouscomponents.¶¶¶¶КакиетипыфильтровсуществуютвASP.NETCore?¶¶Filterexecutionorder:Authorization(canshort-circuitthepipeline) =>Resource(canshort-circuitthepipeline) =>Action(before/after) =>Exception =>Result(before/after) =>Resource(after).¶¶On[Stage]Executingrunscodebeforethestage;¶On[Stage]Executedrunscodeafterthestage;¶¶Authorizationfilters (`IAuthorizationFilter`) —runfirstandcheckwhethertheuserisauthorizedtoperformtherequest;canimmediatelyterminatethepipelineiftheuserisnotauthorized.¶¶Resourcefilters (`IResourceFilter`) —runbeforeanyactionfiltersorafterallfilters.Usefulforcaching,processingrequestsandresponses,orhandlingexceptions.¶¶Actionfilters (`IActionFilter`) —appliedtocontrollermethods,runsbeforeandafterthecontrolleractionmethodexecution.Usedforlogging,requestvalidation,andmodifyingresponses.¶¶Exceptionfilter (`IExceptionFilter`) —runsifanunhandledexceptionoccursinsidetheactionmethod,butbeforetheActionResultisexecuted.¶¶Resultfilters (`IResultFilter`) —runbeforeandaftertheactionresultexecutesiftheactionmethodcompletessuccessfully.AppliedtotheresultsofcontrollermethodsandRazorPages.Usedforformattingthemethodresult,addingcustomheaders,etc.¶¶EndpointFiltersandRouteHandlerFilter —specialfiltersavailableonlyinMinimalAPIs (forrequest/responsepipelinecustomizationspecifictoendpoints).¶¶¶¶ВчемразницамеждуContinuouslyIntegration,ContinuousdeliveryиContinuousdeployment¶¶Continuousintegrationisasoftwaredevelopmentpracticeinwhichcodechangesareregularlymergedintothemainbranchoftherepository.Witheachmerge,theprojectisautomaticallybuiltandthefullsetoftestsisrun.¶¶ContinuousdeliveryisanextensionofCI,butaftersuccessfulbuildandtesting,thechangesarenotjuststoredintherepository —theyareautomaticallydeployedtoatestand/orproductionenvironmentandpreparedfordeploymenttoproduction.However,deployingtoproductionusuallyrequiresmanualapproval.¶¶Continuousdeploymentistheevolutionofdelivery,whereeverystepisperformedautomatically,includingdeploymenttoproduction,andonlyafailedtestwillstopthedeployment.Thus,anyfeatureorfixthatpassesthepipelinebecomesinstantlyavailabletoclients.¶¶¶¶Cleanarchitecture¶¶Cleanarchitectureconsistsofthefollowinglayers:¶ -Presentation —theoutermostlayerusedbytheuseroraclientapplication (WebAPI)¶ -Controllers —controllers.¶¶ -Core —theinnerbusinesslayer:¶ -Application —thisiswherebusinesslogic,requesthandling,andvalidationareimplemented.The `DbContext`interfaceoranyinfrastructuredependenciesaredefinedhere,whichareimplementedbelowin `Infrastructure`.ThisiswhereCQRSisoftenapplied.¶ -Common —containsinfrastructure,i.e.,everythingrelatedtobehavior,exceptions,interfaces,mapping,etc.¶ -Cqrs —containscommandsandqueriesforallentities,consistingofCommandandCommandHandler.¶ -TheMediatRlibraryisusedhere.¶ -Domain —containsbusinessmodels:¶ -Entities —entities (tables,aggregates).¶ -Dto —containsasetofEntityDto —lightweightmodelsfortransferringdatabetweenlayers,andcanalsobeusedformapping.¶¶ -Infrastructure —everythingrelatedtoexternalimplementationdetails,suchasdatabases:¶ -Persistence —thelayerresponsibleforinteractingwiththedatabase;implementstheinterfacesthat `Application`definesasabstractions.Itreferences `Application`and `Domain`,butnotviceversa.¶ -Configurations —defineFluentAPIconfigurationsforworkingwithentities (`Domain.Entities`).¶¶¶¶Примерыиреализациипаттерновповедения.¶¶Behavioralpatternsareliketheater:theydescribetherolesandinteractionsofactorsonthestage.¶¶Strategy —isafamilyofalgorithmsthatcanbeinterchangedatruntime,achievedthroughanabstractionorinterface.Example:anavigatorwithstrategies (onfoot,bycar,publictransport)thatcanbeusedtoreachadestination.¶¶Iteratorallowsyoutosequentiallytraverseacompositeobjectasasinglecollectionwithoutexposingitsinternalstructure.Forexample,duringasightseeing,youcanchooseyourownorderofvisitingattractionswithyourownroutinglogic,oryoucanuseaguide.¶¶Mediator —reducescouplingamongmanyclassesbymovingtheircommunicationintoasinglemediatorclass.Objectsdonotcommunicatewitheachotherdirectly,theyinteractthroughthemediator.Forexample,airplanepilotsdonotcommunicatewitheachotherdirectlytocoordinateflights;instead,thereisadispatcherwhocoordinatesthem.InMVC,thecontrolleractsasamediatorbetweentheviewandthemodel.¶¶¶¶Примерыиреализациипорождающихпаттернов.¶¶Creationalpatternsarelikeakitchen:theydescribetherecipe/processofcreatingobjects.¶¶AbstractFactoryallowsyoutocreatefamiliesofrelatedobjectswithoutspecifyingtheirconcreteclasses.Forexample,anabstractfurniturefactoryandaproductlineoffurniturepresentedindifferentstyles;GUIelementslikebuttons,windows,etc.,whichmaylookdifferentondifferentoperatingsystems.¶¶FactoryMethoddefinesacommoninterfaceforcreatingobjects,buttheactualobjectsarecreatedbysubclasses.Forexample,therearedifferenttypesoffans,buteachspecificfanisbuiltbyitsownfactory.¶¶Builderallowsyoutocreatecomplexobjectsstepbystep.Itenablesyoutoassembledifferentcombinationsofthefinalobjectfromitsparts.Forexample,buildingdifferenthousesthatconsistofthesameconstructionstages,butusedifferentmaterials.¶¶¶¶Примерыиреализацииструктурныхпаттернов.¶¶StructuralpatternsarelikeLEGOblocks:theydescribethestructureofasystem.¶¶Adapterallowsyoutoconverttheinterfaceofoneclassintoanotherinterface.Itenablesobjectswithincompatibleinterfacestoworktogetherwithoutmodifyingtheircode.Forexample,aEuropeanchargerneedsanadaptertofitaUSoutlet;oryouhavedatainXMLformat,butyouneedtoconvertittoJSON.¶¶Decoratorallowsyoutodynamicallyaddnewfunctionalitytoobjectsbywrappingthem.Forexample,apersoncanputondifferentclothes,whereeachpieceofclothinggivesdifferentbonuses.¶¶Facadeprovidesasimpleinterfacetoacomplexsystemofclassesorlibraries.Forexample,asupportservice —anoperatorwillcontactthenecessarypeopletosolveyourproblem;generatingaPDFmightrequire 10steps,but `PdfFacade`canexposeasingle `GenerateReport()`method.¶¶¶¶ВчемотличиеIDE,Runtimeиsdk¶¶IDE (IntegratedDevelopmentEnvironment)isagraphicaldevelopmentenvironmentthatprovidesdeveloperswithtoolscollectedinasingleapplicationforwriting,editing,compiling,debugging,andtestingcode.Itincludesacodeeditor,compilers,debuggers,andothertoolsthatsimplifythedevelopmentprocess.¶¶SDK (SoftwareDevelopmentKit) —asetoftoolsandlibrariesfordevelopingapplications.The .NETSDKincludestoolsforbuilding,publishing,running,andcompilingapplicationstotranslatehigh-levelcodeintoCIL,aswellastheruntime (for .NET,that\'stheCommonLanguageRuntime,CLR).¶¶Runtime —theCommonLanguageRuntime (CLR)virtualmachinerequiredtorunanapplication;itconvertsCILintomachineinstructionsusingaJust-In-Time (JIT)compiler.¶¶¶¶ВчемразницаизачемнужныIActionResult,ActionResult.¶¶IActionResult,ActionResult,ActionResult<T> -theyallowcontrollerstoreturnactionresultsandcanbeusedtoreturnresultsfrombothsynchronousandasynchronousactions.¶¶IActionResultisaninterfacethatisimplementedbyallresulttypes.Itprovidescreatingcustomresulttypes.Italsoprovidesvariousmethodssuchas `Ok()`, `BadRequest()`, `NotFound()`, `Redirect()`,etc.,whichcanbeusedtoreturnstandardHTTPresults.¶¶ActionResultisthebaseclassforallreturnableresulttypes.Itprovidesthesamecapabilitiesastheinterface,butalsoallowsreturningconcreteobjects/modelsThisletsyouomitthetypein `[ProducesResponseType(200)]` (insteadof `[ProducesResponseType(200,Type =typeof(Thing))]`,whichSwaggerusestounderstandwhatobjectisreturned),andwrite `returnsomeObject;`insteadof `returnOk(someObject);`.¶¶ActionResult<T> —letsyouexplicitlyspecifytheexpectedreturntype,soSwagger/OpenAPIautomaticallyunderstandtheresponseschemaandstatuscodes,eliminatingtheneedtomanuallyspecify `[ProducesResponseType(...)]`.¶¶¶¶Почемуstringimmutable¶¶Thismeansthatafterastringiscreated,itscontentscannotbechanged;youcanonlycreateanewstringwiththemodifiedvalue.¶Thisprovidesthefollowingadvantages:¶-Safetyinmultithreadedenvironments:immutablestringssimplifyconcurrencybecauseonethreadcannotchangeastringwhileanotherisusingit.Accordingly,there\'snoneedtosynchronizeaccessacrossthreads.¶-Threadsafety:stringscanbepassedbetweendifferentpartsofaprogramwithconfidencethattheirvaluewillremainthesame.¶-Memoryoptimization:Sincestringsareimmutable,thecompilercanreusethesamestringindifferentpartsoftheprogramwithoutcreatingduplicates.¶-Stringinterning:Becausestringsareimmutable,thecompilercanstorethemintheinternpool,whichhelpssavememoryandimproveperformance.¶-Easeofuse:adeveloperdoesn\'thavetothinkaboutastring\'s \"state\" —it\'salwaysstableandpredictable.¶¶¶¶Недостаткимикросервисов¶¶Infrastructurecomplexity —Microservicearchitectureinvolvesmanyservices,eachofwhichrequiresmanagement,increasinginfrastructurecomplexity.¶Deploymentandscaling —unlikeamonolith,individualservicescanbescaledindependentlyratherthanthewholeapplication.ThisrequiresskillsinDevOps,containerization (Docker),orchestration (K8s),etc.¶¶Networkinteractionsandfaulttolerance —Sinceservicesinteractwitheachotheroverthenetwork,issueslikelatency,packetloss,orfailurescancauseperformanceanddataconsistencyproblems.Additionalresiliencepatternsmayneedtobeimplemented:Retry (reattemptfailedrequests),CircuitBreaker (disabling \"broken\"services),Bulkheadisolation (isolateworkloadstoavoidcascadingfailures),Timeout (timelimits),Fallback (provideanalternativewhenaservicefails),LoadShedding (dropnon-criticalrequestsunderheavyload),Cache (reducerepeatedexpensivecalls).¶¶Dataconsistency —Inamonolith,oneglobaldatabasetransactioncanencapsulateeverythingunder `ACID`rules,butInmicroservices,eachserviceusuallyhasitsowndatabase,soasingleglobaltransactionisnotpossible.Therefore,patternslikeSaga,EventSourcing,CQRS,andeventualconsistencyareneeded.¶¶DevelopmentProcess -microservicesrequirecleardefinitionofserviceboundaries,interfaceagreements,andmaintainingcompatibility,whichcancomplicatethedevelopmentprocess.¶¶Monitoringanddebuggingbecomemoredifficultduetotheneedtotrackmultiplecomponents,andintegrationandsystemtestingaremorecomplexbecauseoftheinteractionbetweenmanyservices.', 1);
